WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.949
LLM API design should make request and response structure explicit.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:07.797
A chat endpoint usually accepts system, user, and assistant messages,

00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:09.721
model selection parameters, generation settings,

00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:12.415
and possibly tool or structured output options.

00:00:12.415 --> 00:00:15.494
A completion endpoint may accept a single prompt.

00:00:15.494 --> 00:00:19.343
An embeddings endpoint usually accepts text and returns vector representations.

00:00:19.343 --> 00:00:23.576
Clear schemas help clients send predictable requests and handle responses safely.

00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:27.810
Generation settings should be treated as part of the API contract.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.888
Temperature, sampling controls, max output length, stop conditions,

00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:33.582
and context limits can affect behavior dramatically.

00:00:33.582 --> 00:00:37.046
Some applications should expose these settings to trusted operators,

00:00:37.046 --> 00:00:39.355
while others should enforce server-side defaults.

00:00:39.355 --> 00:00:41.280
If clients can change everything,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.128
troubleshooting becomes difficult and safety assumptions may no longer hold.

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:50.901
Streaming responses are useful when users benefit from seeing partial output as it is generated.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:54.750
Streaming can improve perceived responsiveness, but it also complicates cancellation,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.828
timeout behavior, logging, error handling, and client code.

00:00:57.828 --> 00:01:02.447
A robust API should define what happens when a request is canceled,

00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:05.526
when the model stalls, when the client disconnects,

00:01:05.526 --> 00:01:08.989
or when the service cannot finish within expected limits.

00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:10.913
Structured output expectations need validation.

00:01:10.913 --> 00:01:15.147
If an application asks the model to return JSON, a classification,

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:16.686
or an action plan,

00:01:16.686 --> 00:01:21.689
the downstream system should still verify that the output is well formed, complete,

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:23.229
and appropriate for use.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:25.923
Models are not parsers or authorization engines.

00:01:25.923 --> 00:01:30.541
Version API behavior as models and prompts change so clients do not

00:01:30.541 --> 00:01:33.620
silently inherit incompatible output formats or changed semantics.
